<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portsmouth, New Hampshire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Strawbery Banke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portsmouth is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piscataqua-river">Piscataqua River</a>. Settled around 1630 as Strawbery Banke, it took its present name in 1653 and was the colonial capital of New Hampshire. It prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, shipbuilding and trade, and the treaty ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> was negotiated there in 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire.jpg" alt="Portsmouth, New Hampshire" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quintin Soloviev · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portsmouth,_NH.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piscataqua-river">Piscataqua River</a> is a tidal estuary with a swift current, but it forms a good natural harbour, and it is the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> and Maine. The first European to explore and write about the area was Martin Pring in 1603. Colonists settled the west bank in 1630 and named the place Strawbery Banke after the wild strawberries growing there. Fort William and Mary, on what is now New Castle Island, protected the village. Fishing, lumber and shipbuilding were the principal businesses, and the port prospered as a link between upstream industries and merchants abroad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Enslaved Africans were imported as labourers as early as 1645 and were integral to the city's prosperity, and Portsmouth took part in the Triangle Trade. The town was incorporated in 1653 and named after the colony's founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-mason-connecticut">John Mason</a>, who had been captain of the English port of Portsmouth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. Its shipyard completed its first ship for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, HMS Falkland, in 1696. After Queen Anne's War, Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-dudley">Joseph Dudley</a> chose the town to host the negotiations for the 1713 Treaty of Portsmouth, which temporarily ended hostilities between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> and the colonies of Massachusetts Bay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>.</p><p>In 1774 Paul Revere rode to Portsmouth to warn that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was coming to capture the port, and the Patriot government later moved the capital inland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter-new-hampshire">Exeter</a>. In 1779 nineteen enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> of Portsmouth petitioned the state legislature for the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, citing the principles of the Revolution; the legislature tabled it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> abolished slavery in 1857. Portsmouth was incorporated as a city in 1849. A fire in 1813 burned 244 buildings, and the fire district created afterwards required brick walls and slate roofs, which gave the downtown its distinctive look. As mill towns grew on river power, Portsmouth's port declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Portsmouth is a city in Rockingham County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>, with a population of 21,956 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It keeps many Colonial, Georgian and Federal style houses, some now museums, and a Historic District Commission protects its architecture. The Captain Gregory Purcell house, where John Paul Jones boarded in 1781–1782 while his ship Ranger was built on Badger's Island in Kittery, is the only surviving property in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> associated with him. The Portsmouth Naval Shipyard, founded in 1800 as the first federal navy yard, stands on Seavey's Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kittery-maine">Kittery, Maine</a>, and was where the 1905 treaty ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> was signed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portsmouth, New Hampshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fishing settlements at Dover and <b>Portsmouth</b>, then called Strawbery Banke, appeared on the Piscataqua River from 1623.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-hampshire">Province of New Hampshire</a></li></ul></section>
